// Settings of the simulation itself (WP4.2, WP4.3, WP4.4), as opposed to the definitions of
// its units. Read by sf_data::sim_settings; sf_bridge hands them to the world.
// The rules they belong to are in docs/rules/pathfinding.md and docs/rules/collision.md.
//
// path_node_budget: how many nodes of path search work the simulation may do in
// one tick, for all units together. Every search step that expands a cell or a
// portal counts one. A bigger budget gives units their routes sooner (a crowd
// that is ordered at once is served in fewer ticks); it never changes which
// route a unit gets. It costs time in every tick the service has work, so it is
// the knob that trades the load of a big order against the frame time.
// At least 512 (a line of sight, the one step that cannot be cut, needs up to 60).
// Tests and scenarios that do not give a budget use the simulation's own
// default, which is the same number.
//
// flowfield_threshold: how many units of one movement class a single order must send to
// one place for the simulation to build one flowfield for them (WP4.3) instead of
// routing each on its own. A smaller number makes more orders groups: a field costs
// about as much as a handful of single routes and then serves any number of units,
// but it is built for one goal and a region, so a pair of units sent to different
// places is better off with a route each. 0 turns flowfields off (every unit is routed
// on its own); 1 is not a group and is refused; at most 1000.
// Tests and scenarios that do not give one use the simulation's own default, which is
// the same number.
//
// collision (WP4.4): whether units have bodies. A body is a disc of half the unit's `size`
// (unit_defs.ron); bodies are pushed apart, units that meet head-on keep right, a unit
// holding position is never pushed. false lets units pass through each other, as before WP4.4.
//
// wait_ticks: a unit that has not come closer to where it is going for this many ticks
// detours round what is in its way (a unit holding position, a unit coming the other way).
// 20 ticks are a second. At least 1.
//
// stuck_ticks: a unit that has not come closer for this many ticks steps to a free
// neighbouring spot. More than wait_ticks, at most 1000.
//
// stuck_attempts: after this many such steps in a row it asks for a new route (WP4.7): a
// new search from where it stands that goes round the bodies near it that stand still (a
// unit holding position). At least 1.
// Tests and scenarios that do not give them use the simulation's own defaults, which are
// the same numbers.
//
// replan_cooldown (WP4.7): ticks between two such requests of one unit; it sidesteps again
// meanwhile. 1 to 1000.
//
// replan_max (WP4.7): how many such requests one unit may make in one order. When it is
// stuck still after the last, the order is given up: its next queued order starts, or it
// stands idle where it is. 1 to 100.
//
// retarget_cooldown (WP4.7): ticks between two new searches of a unit that goes for a
// target that keeps moving (an attack, a guard of an entity). 1 to 1000.
//
// Formations (WP4.6, docs/rules/formations.md): one move order that sends enough units that
// cross the same ground (foot, wheeled and tracked units are one family; hovercraft and vessels
// each their own) to one place makes them a formation: they keep the shape they stand in, as a
// lattice with room for their bodies, turned with the way they go, at the pace of the slowest;
// narrow places break the shape up and open ground forms it again. Single orders, attack-move,
// hold, stop and scatter are not touched.
//
// formation: false moves a group as a group order did before WP4.6.
//
// formation_min_units / formation_max_units: how many units of one family one order must send
// to form a formation, and the most units one order may send and still make formations (a
// crowd of two hundred forms none, it moves as a group order always did; 19 is the largest
// size the sweep of tools/sf_headless/tests/formation_sweep.rs is clean for, for every shape
// and mix of the group). 2 to 256.
//
// formation_spacing_permille: the width of the lattice, as thousandths of what the widest body
// of the group needs (1000: bodies side by side). 1000 to 3000.
//
// formation_spread_permille: a scattered group is pulled in until it is no wider than this many
// thousandths of the radius of a disc that holds its bodies. 1000 to 4000.
//
// formation_pace_permille: the speed of the formation while some member is behind its slot, as
// thousandths of the current speed of its slowest member. The part that is kept back is what
// the slowest has to catch up with; with nobody behind, the formation goes at the slowest's
// own speed. 500 to 1000.
//
// formation_catchup_permille: the most speed a member uses to get back to its slot, as
// thousandths of its own. Not below the pace, at most 1000.
//
// formation_reform_tenths: how far from its slot (tenths of a cell) a member may be before the
// leader waits for it on open ground. 5 to 100.
//
// formation_patience_ticks: for how many ticks the leader waits for a straggler that does not
// come closer before the formation counts as broken (nobody waits any more until the
// stragglers are back), and for how long a member may be away from its slot without coming
// closer before it leaves. 20 to 2000.
// Tests and scenarios that do not give them use the simulation's own defaults, which are the
// same numbers.
//
// Targeting (WP5.4, docs/rules/targeting.md): an idle unit, a unit on hold, a guard and a unit on
// attack-move pick an enemy within the reach of their weapons, and a unit that is shot at turns on its
// attacker. A world that is not given these settings does none of it.
//
// scan_cooldown: ticks between two looks round for a target of one unit (20 ticks are a second). A
// unit that has a target keeps it and does not look. 1 to 1000.
//
// pursuit_cells: how far beyond its weapon's reach a unit that is shot at follows its attacker, and the
// farthest it strays from where it stood when it began. Only an idle unit and a guard follow; a unit on
// hold or on attack-move fires back only from where it is, and one that is moving does not stop. 0
// means nobody follows. At most 32.
// Tests and scenarios that do not give them use the simulation's own defaults, which are the same
// numbers.
(
    path_node_budget: 4096,
    flowfield_threshold: 8,
    collision: true,
    wait_ticks: 6,
    stuck_ticks: 60,
    stuck_attempts: 3,
    replan_cooldown: 100,
    replan_max: 3,
    retarget_cooldown: 20,
    formation: true,
    formation_min_units: 3,
    formation_max_units: 19,
    formation_spacing_permille: 1250,
    formation_spread_permille: 1500,
    formation_pace_permille: 900,
    formation_catchup_permille: 1000,
    formation_reform_tenths: 30,
    formation_patience_ticks: 300,
    scan_cooldown: 10,
    pursuit_cells: 6,
)
